<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Piedmontese Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1798 CE – 1799 CE · capital Turin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The provisional republic set up in Piedmont after the French forced King Charles Emmanuel IV to abandon the mainland in 1798.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In December 1798 French troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-emmanuel-iv-of-sardinia">Charles Emmanuel IV</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia</a> to give up his mainland states and withdraw to the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. A provisional government of Piedmontese patriots took over in Turin, proclaiming a republic.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The provisional government abolished feudal rights and noble titles and held votes in which the Piedmontese communities asked to be annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>; the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-first-republic">French Directory</a> approved the annexation in early 1799. Real power lay with French commissioners and generals, and French requisitions caused unrest in the countryside.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1799 the Austro-Russian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-suvorov">Suvorov</a> drove the French out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, ending the regime. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marengo">Marengo</a> in 1800, the French set up the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/subalpine-republic">Subalpine Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/piedmontese-republic.jpg" alt="Piedmontese Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Vanderall · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piedmont-Subalpine_Flag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1798 CE – 1799 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Turin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Piedmontese, Italian, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Repubblica Piemontese</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1798&amp;focus=piedmontese-republic" aria-label="Piedmontese Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4863 586 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Piedmontese Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5183 842L5140 827M5183 842L5054 728M5183 842L5183 842"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5140 827h0M5054 728h0M5183 842h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5183 842h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5183 842h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Turin. <a href="/atlas?year=1798&amp;focus=piedmontese-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
